    <body>
      <sec id="article-25023.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Mesial temporal lobe epilepsy is the most common form of focal epilepsy. This course reviews the historical foundations, etiologic diversity, semiology, diagnostic evaluation, and contemporary management strategies of this condition. The unique clinical features of mesial temporal seizures, including auras, automatisms, and postictal phenomena,&#x000a0;and characteristic findings on scalp and invasive electroencephalography and advanced neuroimaging are also discussed. The activity also&#x000a0;examines the high prevalence of pharmacoresistance in this population and the critical role of accurate epileptogenic zone localization in guiding surgical and minimally invasive therapies, neurostimulation, and comprehensive care.</p>
        <p>This course addresses the cognitive, psychiatric, and structural brain comorbidities associated with mesial temporal lobe epilepsy to underscore the need for&#x000a0;interprofessional management.&#x000a0;Participants will also gain an understanding of the pathophysiology and genetics of mesial temporal lobe epilepsy, the interpretation of seizure semiology and diagnostic evaluation, and medical, surgical, and neurostimulation treatment options.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in identifying mesial temporal lobe epilepsy, performing the recommended evaluation, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach to manage this condition, thereby improving patient outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the etiologic mechanisms of mesial temporal lobe epilepsy.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Interpret electroencephalogram data to evaluate patients with mesial temporal lobe epilepsy.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select evidence-based antiseizure therapies tailored to individual characteristics in patients with mesial temporal lobe epilepsy.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate on interprofessional treatment strategies to improve care coordination and outcomes in patients with mesial temporal lobe epilepsy.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=25023&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=25023">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The temporal lobes are the most common region of the brain to develop&#x000a0;epileptogenicity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Historically, "uncinate fits" were first described by Hughlings Jackson in the 19th century, linking seizures presenting as "dreamy states" to lesions in the uncus of the temporal lobe. In the mid-20th century,&#x000a0;the term "psychomotor epilepsy" was introduced by Gibbs and Gibbs to describe the characteristic psychic and emotional semiological findings of&#x000a0;patients with seizures arising from the temporal lobes and their associated electrophysiologic features.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;These experiential phenomena were later studied by Jasper and Penfield using&#x000a0;intraoperative stimulation experiments and confirmed to arise from the temporal lobes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>Mesial temporal lobe epilepsy (MTLE) is recognized as a distinct clinical entity because it differs from lateral temporal lobe epilepsy in etiology, semiology, imaging findings, and electrophysiologic characteristics. The mesial temporal structures account for nearly 80% of all temporal lobe epilepsy cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients who progress to medically refractory epilepsy may benefit from surgical intervention. Epilepsy surgeries, including temporal lobectomy and laser interstitial thermal therapy, offer effective therapeutic options for carefully selected individuals with drug-resistant disease.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Most cases of MTLE&#x000a0;are sporadic and&#x000a0;occur as an&#x000a0;aftereffect&#x000a0;of neuronal injury from perinatal hypoxia, prolonged febrile status epilepticus<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r5">[5]</xref>, malformations of cortical development, autoimmune encephalitis, and CNS infection. However, many recent studies have&#x000a0;suggested&#x000a0;the role of the genetic background in the development of epilepsy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;The current perspective is that mesial temporal structures&#x000a0;become more susceptible to injury when mutations occur in specific&#x000a0;transcription factors associated with neurodevelopmental genes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>On the other hand, familial forms&#x000a0;are not uncommon.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;One study showed that as high as one-fifth of the newly diagnosed non-lesional MTLE could have a familial attribute. Research has identified a genetic locus for familial MTLE in a large family with an autosomal dominant MTLE phenotype.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;The familial MTLE cases have been shown to exhibit a complex inheritance pattern and usually do not demonstrate mesial temporal sclerosis on imaging.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Hippocampal sclerosis is the most common histopathological abnormality found in patients with drug-resistant temporal lobe epilepsy. In a European series of 9523&#x000a0;patients with epilepsy undergoing surgery,&#x000a0;hippocampal sclerosis was identified in 36.4%, long-term epilepsy-associated tumors in 23.6%, and focal cortical dysplasias in 19.8%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;Focal cortical dysplasias are classified as malformations of cortical development and include polymicrogyria, nodular heterotopia, and hamartomas, which are less common pathologies seen in temporal lobe epilepsy. Other less common etiologies include postinfectious (most commonly after HSV encephalitis), vascular malformations, ischemic lesions, inflammatory lesions, and traumatic encephalomalacia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>MTLE&#x000a0;demonstrates no specific age or sex predominance. Affected individuals typically report a normal perinatal history and normal developmental milestones. Neurological examination findings generally remain normal, and cognitive function is often preserved at presentation. A childhood history of febrile seizures serves as an important predictor for later development of MTLE.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>A prospective study of 226 children with febrile status epilepticus (FEBSTAT) identified acute hippocampal injury in 9.7% of participants. Follow-up brain MRI in 14 of the 22 affected patients revealed hippocampal sclerosis in 10 and hippocampal volume loss in 12.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r16">[16]</xref> Additionally, less significant risk factors include head trauma, birth trauma, childhood central nervous system infection, and posterior cerebral artery territory infarcts.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s5" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Hippocampal sclerosis is histopathologically characterized by segmental pyramidal cell loss in the CA1, CA3, and CA4 regions, whereas CA2 pyramidal and dentate gyrus granule cells are most seizure-resistant.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;Neuronal cell loss is associated with reactive astrogliosis, leading to tissue stiffening, which has traditionally been termed&#x000a0;"Ammon's horn sclerosis."<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;Some of the proposed&#x000a0;mechanisms include disruption of neuronal circuitry, leading to aberrant mossy fiber sprouting and molecular rearrangements or plasticity in ion channel and neurotransmitter receptor expression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;Abnormalities have also been noted&#x000a0;in&#x000a0;the&#x000a0;dentate gyrus in the form of granule cell dispersion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Additionally, variable cell loss is also detectable in adjacent cortical regions, including the subiculum, entorhinal cortex, and amygdala.</p>
        <p>Several classification systems have been proposed for hippocampal sclerosis.&#x000a0;The most widely used classification system is the International League Against Epilepsy (ILAE) classification, which divides hippocampal sclerosis into&#x000a0;3 types based on a semiquantitative survey of segmental cell loss within hippocampal subfields. ILAE type 1 has both CA1 and CA4 loss; ILAE type 2 has predominant CA1 loss, and ILAE type 3 has predominant CA4 loss.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>Malformations of cortical development represent a wide range of cortical lesions resulting from the derangement of normal developmental processes involving cells implicated in the formation of the cortical mantle.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Focal cortical dysplasias represent the most common type of malformations of cortical development and characteristically demonstrate disruption of normal cortical lamination by the presence of large aberrant neurons, as well as "grotesque cells" in both cortex and subcortical white matter.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>Gangliogliomas and dysembryoplastic neuroepithelial tumors are the most frequent long-term epilepsy-associated tumors, comprising 65% of brain tumors encountered in patients undergoing epilepsy surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r26">[26]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>
<bold>Seizure Semiology</bold>
</p>
        <p>Based on the revision of the ILAE classification system, patients with MTLE are usually classified as having focal epilepsy and manifest as focal seizures with preserved or impaired consciousness, and may progress to bilateral&#x000a0;tonic-clonic seizures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>When preserved consciousness is present, the patient can describe unusual sensations known as auras. Auras can be visceral, autonomic, gustatory, or affective symptoms. Patients commonly experience a rising epigastric sensation, nausea, and olfactory or gustatory hallucinations. Psychic sensations can include depersonalization (out-of-body feeling), d&#x000e9;j&#x000e0; vu (a sense of familiarity), <italic toggle="yes">jamais vu</italic> (feeling of unfamiliarity), <italic toggle="yes">d&#x000e9;j&#x000e0; entendu</italic> (hearing familiar sounds), or panoramic visions (a rapid recollection of past episodes). Dysphoric or euphoric feelings, fear, terror, anger, and other sensations can also occur. Often, the patients find the aura hard to describe.</p>
        <p>Autonomic features include piloerection, pallor or flushing, tachycardia, or pupillary dilatation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Another early semiological sign of mesial temporal seizure onset is ictal central apnea (ICA). However, additional multimodal respiratory monitoring with thoracic-abdominal belts would be necessary to detect ICA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;With loss of consciousness, patients exhibit behavioral arrest and a blank, staring facial appearance, followed by the development of oral, facial, and alimentary automatisms, eg, lip-smacking, chewing, sucking, or swallowing. This is&#x000a0;usually accompanied by ipsilateral gestural automatisms, eg, repetitive hand movements, picking, and fidgeting behavior, disrobing, and contralateral dystonic posturing of limbs. The presence of speech disturbances suggests nondominant hemispheric seizure onset, but its&#x000a0;absence is not a reliable lateralizing finding. Patients commonly have a period of postictal confusion following the episode. Less commonly, episodes may progress to bilateral&#x000a0;tonic-clonic seizures.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Although the advent of antiepileptic medications&#x000a0;has improved the quality of life of patients with epilepsy by reducing seizure frequency, many of the patients with MTLE have a greater tendency to become pharmacoresistant over time. Studies have shown that less than 25% of patients with MTLE remained seizure-free for greater than&#x000a0;1 year.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;This data elucidates the importance of nonpharmacological therapies in patients having MTLE. However, most of these nonpharmacological modalities require accurate identification of the epileptogenic zone to provide successful seizure outcomes.</p>
        <p>Successful implementation of such therapies depends on precise identification of the epileptogenic zone, defined as the minimum cortex requiring inactivation, resection, or disconnection to achieve seizure freedom.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;However, the epileptogenic zone is a theoretical construct, and its identification requires careful integration of all available information sources. These sources include electrophysiologic data obtained from electroencephalography (EEG) and magnetoencephalography (MEG) that have a good temporal resolution and various neuroimaging modalities, eg, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), interictal positron emission tomography (PET), ictal single-photon emission tomography (SPECT), subtracted ictal SPECT coregistered to MRI (SISCOM), and functional MRI that have a good spatial resolution. Neuropsychological assessment is also employed as a part of a presurgical evaluation to evaluate the functional characteristics of the affected epileptogenic region. Such an extensive evaluation can be performed most effectively at&#x000a0;a comprehensive epilepsy center staffed by a cohesive team of specialists trained in neurology, neurosurgery, neuroradiology, neuropsychology, neuropathology, and psychiatric liaison services.</p>
        <p>High-resolution 3T/7T MRI of the brain with thin cuts through the temporal lobes is a powerful tool for assessing subtle structural abnormalities involving the mesial temporal structures. Additional information regarding hippocampal pathology can be obtained using multiple MR modalities,&#x000a0;eg, volumetry, spectroscopy, and diffusion tensor imaging (DTI).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;Interictal PET evaluates metabolism, specifically hypometabolism, and can identify the epileptogenic temporal lobe in up to 70% to 90% of patients with MTLE.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;On the other hand, ictal SPECT/SISCOM evaluates perfusion, specifically areas of hyperperfusion, and is also a useful tool, especially for assessing the origin and spread along the epileptogenic network.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>The goal of presurgical evaluation for epilepsy surgery is to accurately lateralize and localize the seizure focus; this includes phase 1 and phase 2 evaluations.&#x000a0;Phase 1 evaluations use noninvasive modalities to determine where the seizure starts, including video-EEG, MEG, MRI, interictal PET, ictal SPECT/SISCOM, and neuropsychological assessment. Patients with temporal lobe epilepsy involving the dominant temporal lobe also need functional MRI and intracarotid amobarbital/methohexital (Wada) test for language and memory lateralization.</p>
        <p>Phase 2 evaluations include the use of surgically placed electrodes directly over the brain parenchyma to determine where exactly the seizure is originating. This phase involves the use of invasive techniques, eg, placement of subdural grids and strips or depth electrode placement for electrocorticography (ECoG) and stereo-electroencephalography (SEEG).</p>
        <p>
<bold>Scalp Electroencephalogram&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>The EEG background in patients with mesial temporal lobe epilepsy is usually normal. There may be periods of intermittent slowing noted in the anterior temporal EEG derivations that become prominent during sleep and hyperventilation and are suggestive of focal cerebral dysfunction. Sometimes the focal slowing can be more robust and manifests as temporal intermittent rhythmic delta activity (TIRDA).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>In addition to slowing, the classic interictal EEG abnormality in MTLE is the presence of spikes or sharp waves that phase reverse over the anterior temporal regions. The dipole orientation of these sharp waves appears to have maximum electronegativity and voltage in the basal temporal derivations (T8/T9; FT8/FT9),&#x000a0;and electropositivity is distributed widely in the contralateral centro-parietal derivations (C3/C4; P3/P4).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;The sharp waves in the anterior temporal region&#x000a0;are present in the majority of patients with MTLE.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;They tend to occur more frequently during drowsiness and the early stages of sleep.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r39">[39]</xref> The sharp waves in the anterior temporal region become less frequent during REM sleep and are somewhat similar in frequency to those seen during awake periods.</p>
        <p>Ictal EEG findings in patients with MTLE&#x000a0;are unique when compared to neocortical epilepsy because of their gradual, rhythmic build-up and delayed spread to neighboring brain regions. The seizures that arise from the hippocampus usually spread to the basal temporal regions. Therefore, the use of sphenoidal electrodes can be beneficial for detecting the ictal onset in many cases of this seizure type. The characteristic pattern seen at the onset of an MTL seizure is rhythmic theta activity that starts at the anterior/anterior-inferior temporal or sphenoidal electrode contacts, with gradual spread to the lateral temporal, insular, and frontal regions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>An important localizing feature that can sometimes be observed is diffuse EEG attenuation and cessation of interictal epileptiform discharges (IEDs) at the onset of the seizure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;Shortly after the onset, a slower rhythmic theta build-up or organized spiking is noted, which gradually evolves in frequency and amplitude until the seizure spreads to neighboring brain regions, followed by spread to the contralateral hemisphere. When the EEG onset precedes the clinical onset, the ipsilateral hemisphere is the most likely localization for the seizure onset, with a probability close to 95%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r41">[41]</xref>&#x000a0;Finally, studies have found that postictal slowing is an important lateralizing feature in up to 70% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r37">[37]</xref></p>
        <p>In contrast, an ictal onset with unilateral delta slowing and repetitive interictal spiking is less likely to arise from the mesial temporal region.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;Also, seizure onset with bilateral rhythmic activity and delayed evolution into a temporal pattern is a poor indicator of seizures arising from the mesial temporal region.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Invasive Electroencephalogram</bold>
</p>
        <p>The use of invasive intracranial recordings with subdural grids or intracerebral depth electrodes has improved our diagnostic precision in identifying the seizure focus. Although MTLE primarily involves the temporal lobes, the abnormal network is known to have widespread extra-temporal connectivity. Ruling out other potential nodes in the network that can be independently epileptogenic is essential. Stereo-electroencephalography (SEEG) is an important tool that records electrical activity from very confined, deep-seated brain regions that usually escape detection by common surface recording modalities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;Complex signal processing techniques have been employed to understand the intrinsic properties of epileptogenic networks from electrophysiological signals obtained from SEEG data.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r44">[44]</xref>&#x000a0;SEEG can provide a more comprehensive understanding of the epileptogenic network in MTLE and guide the surgical plan.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r45">[45]</xref></p>
        <p>Unlike in scalp recordings, ictal activity recorded from intracranial electrodes typically manifests as a largely focal or regional fast beta or gamma rhythm. A study identified 5 ictal onset patterns on SEEG in patients with temporal lobe epilepsy, including low-voltage fast activity (LVFA), spike-and-wave activity, low-frequency high-amplitude periodic spikes (LFPS), a burst of high-amplitude polyspikes, and rhythmic sharp activity at 13 Hz.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r46">[46]</xref>&#x000a0;The focality of the rhythm on depth recording is directly proportional to the degree of hippocampal pathology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r47">[47]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>The first-line therapy for MTLE includes the initiation of appropriately chosen&#x000a0;antiseizure medications (ASM) treatment. For patients with MTLE, the most effective ASMs&#x000a0;are those used&#x000a0;to treat focal epilepsies,&#x000a0;eg, carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine, levetiracetam, lamotrigine, and topiramate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;These agents can be monotherapy or, more often, in combination to achieve adequate seizure control. However, patients with MTLE are often known to have an inadequate response to antiepileptic drug therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;Some patients who initially respond may also end up becoming medically refractory within a few years. Nonpharmacological approaches eventually play an essential role in the management of patients with medically refractory or drug-resistant MTLE. These include both surgical and neurostimulation approaches.</p>
        <p>Surgical approaches&#x000a0;for MTLE&#x000a0;include&#x000a0;open resection and other minimally invasive techniques. Standard open resective surgery&#x000a0;is considered to be&#x000a0;the most&#x000a0;effective and safe treatment option for temporal lobe epilepsy, with superiority to prolonged medical therapy in terms of long-term outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r49">[49]</xref>&#x000a0;Several surgical procedures&#x000a0;have been&#x000a0;employed,&#x000a0;including&#x000a0;standard anterior temporal lobectomy, anteromesial temporal lobectomy, selective amygdalohippocampectomy,&#x000a0;and temporal pole resection. Resective therapy has demonstrated excellent outcomes, especially when performed early.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Surgical resection offers postoperative seizure freedom at&#x000a0;2 years in 60% to 80% of patients with drug-resistant MTLE, whereas longer-term follow-ups present less favorable results.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r51">[51]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r52">[52]</xref>&#x000a0;Anterior temporal lobectomy is generally safe, and the most common neurologic complication following such resective epilepsy surgery is a minor visual field deficit.</p>
        <p>Advances in our understanding of epileptic networks have improved our ability to better define the epileptogenic zone in patients with epilepsy. The&#x000a0;aim of disrupting epileptic networks&#x000a0;with the smallest possible&#x000a0;surgical lesion has led to the development of minimally invasive surgical techniques.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r53">[53]</xref>&#x000a0;Minimally invasive techniques include stereotactic radiosurgery (SRS), stereotactic radiofrequency thermocoagulation (SRT), laser interstitial thermal therapy, and&#x000a0;MRI-guided focused ultrasound ablation&#x000a0;(FUS).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r54">[54]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r55">[55]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r56">[56]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r57">[57]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r58">[58]</xref>&#x000a0;SRS, using the Gamma Knife and CyberKnife, delivers ionizing radiation to a focal target within mesial temporal structures in MTLE and has shown comparable postoperative seizure freedom compared&#x000a0;to invasive surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r59">[59]</xref>&#x000a0;Similarly, stereo-EEG (SEEG)- guided thermocoagulation and laser interstitial thermal therapy have shown promising developments and have been employed as alternatives to standard resective surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;Studies have shown that pairing these minimally invasive procedures with&#x000a0;intraoperative electrocorticography (iECoG)&#x000a0;may improve&#x000a0;seizure outcomes for patients with&#x000a0;MTLE.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r62">[62]</xref></p>
        <p>Neurostimulation for the treatment of epilepsy includes vagus nerve stimulation (VNS),&#x000a0;responsive neurostimulation (RNS), and deep brain stimulation (DBS).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r63">[63]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r64">[64]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r65">[65]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r66">[66]</xref>&#x000a0;These are generally reserved for patients who are either not candidates for resective&#x000a0;surgery or unwilling to undergo surgery. RNS can be used for patients with bitemporal seizure foci or foci involving eloquent brain regions. Neurostimulation could also be an option for patients who have seizure recurrence&#x000a0;following surgery. In patients with bitemporal epilepsy, long-term ECoG data from the RNS system can provide information enabling identification of the temporal lobe responsible for the majority of the seizures in certain patients; if so, resective surgery may be a consideration in such patients.</p>
        <p>Apart from seizure management, patients with MTLE may have cognitive problems, psychiatric comorbidities, and psychosocial issues.&#x000a0;A comprehensive approach to managing an individual with MTLE must take into account the cognitive and psychiatric comorbidities that often accompany this condition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r68">[68]</xref>&#x000a0;A meta-analysis showed that a&#x000a0;significant proportion of patients with temporal lobe epilepsy with&#x000a0;comorbid psychiatric illnesses showed&#x000a0;improvement in their psychiatric symptoms following&#x000a0;temporal lobe epilepsy surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r69">[69]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The seizures in patients with MTLE share semiological characteristics with other types of epilepsies,&#x000a0;eg, focal seizures arising from the orbito-frontal region, insular-perisylvian region, and temporo-parieto-occipital junction, and are known as Temporal-Plus epilepsy syndromes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r70">[70]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients with absence seizures also manifest with a blank stare and may have subtle automatisms, which can mimic seizures of MTLE.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r71">[71]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients with seizures arising from the insula can sometimes present in a very similar manner to MTLE in terms of epigastric&#x000a0;aura&#x000a0;and oro-alimentary automatisms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r72">[72]</xref>&#x000a0;Some patients with seizures&#x000a0;arising from the temporo-parieto-occipital junction have seizures that rapidly spread anteriorly to the temporal lobes and can produce semiology very similar to MTLE.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r73">[73]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Further, other medical conditions may mimic temporal lobe seizures,&#x000a0;eg, panic attacks, tardive dyskinesia, excessive daytime sleepiness, periodic limb movement disorder, transient psychotic episodes, and psychogenic non-epileptic seizures (PNES).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r74">[74]</xref>&#x000a0;These need to be differentiated using clinical and diagnostic tools, eg, video-EEG monitoring.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of MTLE is variable and depends on multiple factors, with outcomes ranging from spontaneous remission to drug-resistant epilepsy requiring surgical intervention.&#x000a0;In MTLE patients with hippocampal sclerosis who are being treated with&#x000a0;ASMs, long-term seizure freedom rates range from 29% to 41% with medications alone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r75">[75]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r76">[76]</xref>&#x000a0;However, among drug-naive patients with mild mesial temporal lobe epilepsy, approximately 75% can remain seizure-free over extended follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r77">[77]</xref>&#x000a0;Factors that predict poor prognosis in patients with MTLE include earlier age of onset, longer epilepsy duration, history of frequent febrile seizures, and hippocampal sclerosis on MRI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r77">[77]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>MTLE can be associated with multiple complications affecting cognition, psychiatric health, and brain structure.</p>
        <p>Cognitive complications are among the most disabling features of MTLE, with&#x000a0;memory impairment being the hallmark feature.&#x000a0;Patients with&#x000a0;dominant (often left) MTLE commonly have verbal memory deficits, whereas those with non-dominant (often right) MTLE commonly have visual memory deficits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r78">[78]</xref>&#x000a0;However, cognitive dysfunction can extend well beyond memory to include language (naming, fluency), executive function, attention, and processing speed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r79">[79]</xref>&#x000a0;The widespread impairments reflect not only hippocampal damage but also involvement of extrahippocampal structures, including entorhinal cortex, cingulate gyrus, orbitofrontal cortex, and frontal/parietal lobes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r80">[80]</xref></p>
        <p>Psychiatric comorbidities are more common in MTLE compared to other epilepsy syndromes. Depression, anxiety, and interictal dysphoric disorder occur frequently, with rates highest among patients with hippocampal sclerosis and drug-resistant epilepsy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r81">[81]</xref>&#x000a0;The severity of depression inversely correlates with the degree of hippocampal atrophy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r82">[82]</xref></p>
        <p>Structural brain abnormalities are noted to progress over time in patients with MTLE. Beyond hippocampal sclerosis, patients develop widespread gray matter atrophy affecting temporal, frontal, and subcortical regions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r83">[83]</xref>&#x000a0;In older adults with MTLE, the pattern and magnitude of medial temporal atrophy resemble those seen in mild cognitive impairment, raising concerns about accelerated aging and potential Alzheimer's disease comorbidity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r84">[84]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>MTLE is the most common type of epilepsy that starts in a specific part of the brain called the mesial temporal lobe. Patients with temporal lobe epilepsy tend to have recurrent, unprovoked&#x000a0;seizures that can affect their awareness, memory, and behavior.&#x000a0;The most common cause of MTLE is a condition called hippocampal sclerosis, which means scarring in a part of the brain called the hippocampus. This scarring can happen after a brain injury, infection, inflammation, or prolonged seizures earlier in life. In some cases, physicians cannot find a specific cause on brain imaging.</p>
        <p>The MTLE seizures often present with an aura,&#x000a0;eg, d&#x000e9;j&#x000e0; vu or a rising epigastric sensation, which may be followed by loss of awareness, repetitive movements, eg, lip smacking, picking at clothes, or fumbling with objects. Some people may experience convulsive movements with seizures spreading outside the temporal lobe and to both sides of the brain.&#x000a0;Medications are the mainstay of treatment for MTLE, and about&#x000a0;half of patients respond to them.&#x000a0;Clinicians will work with&#x000a0;patients to find the right medication and dose.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r85">[85]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients should be informed of the importance of taking their medication exactly as prescribed. If medications don't control your seizures, your doctor will discuss with you other modalities of treatment,&#x000a0;eg, epilepsy surgery, neuromodulation, and dietary therapies.</p>
        <p>Many individuals with MTLE experience memory impairment, sleep disturbances, anxiety, depression, and mood changes. These comorbidities warrant discussion with a clinician, who can provide appropriate evaluation and management strategies to address cognitive, emotional, and behavioral concerns.&#x000a0;Although uncommon, sudden unexpected death in epilepsy (SUDEP) represents a serious risk for individuals living with epilepsy. Consistent adherence to prescribed antiseizure medications and active efforts to achieve optimal seizure control remain the most effective strategies for reducing this risk.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>MTLE&#x000a0;represents the most common focal epilepsy syndrome and frequently progresses to drug resistance despite appropriate antiseizure medication therapy. Characteristic semiology, distinctive electroencephalographic patterns, and structural or functional abnormalities of mesial temporal structures define this condition. Because pharmacoresistant MTLE often requires advanced diagnostic evaluation and nonpharmacologic therapies, timely referral to a comprehensive epilepsy center remains central to achieving seizure control, preserving cognition, and improving quality of life.</p>
        <p>Optimal management relies on coordinated interprofessional care. Physicians, general practitioners, and advanced practitioners must recognize drug resistance early and initiate referral to level 3 or 4 epilepsy centers, where epileptologists, neurosurgeons, neuroradiologists, neuropsychologists, and psychiatrists collaboratively interpret video-EEG, neuroimaging, and neuropsychological data to guide individualized treatment strategies. Nurses play a key role in patient monitoring, education, and care coordination, while pharmacists ensure appropriate medication selection, dosing, adherence, and surveillance for adverse effects. Effective communication among team members and with patients and families enhances safety, supports shared decision-making, reduces delays in definitive therapy, and improves long-term outcomes, including seizure freedom and reduced SUDEP risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25023.r86">[86]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25023.s14">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
